\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

Applying MH-DIF analysis to six METABENCH benchmarks (27,125 items, 5,227 models), we find 17.9--31.3\% temporal item instability under ETS~C classification---knowledge/reasoning benchmarks are more affected (C\% $\geq 26.9\%$) than procedural ones (C\% $\leq 18.9\%$). The instability is confirmed non-random (random controls $\leq 0.6\%$) and item-specific; a Simpson's paradox in odds ratio regression confirms item properties, not accuracy gains, drive DIF. Web-overlap labels show near-independence with DIF flags (enrichment $\approx 0.96$); our semi-synthetic framework ($\Delta\mathrm{FPR} = 0.000$, $\Delta\mathrm{TPR} = {+}0.508$) provides a controlled alternative.

DIF-flagged items concentrate discriminative information ($z = -8\sigma$ to $-49\sigma$ vs.\ random removal) and exhibit a difficulty--direction interaction ($\rho = -0.597$): capability gains on hard items, erosion on easy ones. DIF is a diagnostic lens, not a quality filter. LLM benchmarks need psychometric monitoring alongside score reporting.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\subsection{Implications for Benchmark Temporal Comparability}
\label{sec:disc_temporal}

The 31\% ETS~C instability on MMLU is robust across thresholds and structural controls (\Cref{sec:structural}), and extends across all six METABENCH benchmarks (C\% $= 17.9$--$31.3\%$, 45--174$\times$ above random baselines; \Cref{tab:cross_benchmark}). Knowledge/reasoning benchmarks show higher instability (C\% $\geq 26.9\%$) than procedural ones (C\% $\leq 18.9\%$), and a Simpson's paradox in odds ratio regression ($\mathrm{OR}: 1.55 \to 0.73$ after controlling for item properties; LR $\chi^2 = 647$) confirms that item properties---not accuracy gains---drive DIF. This does not invalidate these benchmarks as ranking instruments ($\rho \approx 0.997$) but signals measurement drift across model generations.

\subsection{Ground Truth Meta-Critique}
\label{sec:disc_groundtruth}

The near-independence between DIF flags and web-overlap labels (enrichment $= 0.964$, Jaccard $= 0.206$, $\phi = 0.012$; consistent across contamination subtypes) exposes a category error: web-overlap labels identify a condition shared by nearly all MMLU items (global exposure), so evaluating a differential method against them conflates two phenomena. Item-level differential detectors require item-level differential ground truth.

\subsection{DIF as Diagnostic Lens}
\label{sec:disc_diagnostic}

The GSM1K correlation ($r = -0.478$, $p = 0.005$, $N = 33$; \Cref{sec:gsm8k}) provides exploratory evidence that DIF-flagged items capture behavioral change distinct from memorization; partial correlation ($r = -0.385$, $p = 0.027$) after controlling for accuracy confirms this is not an artifact of general model quality.

Item characterization reinforces this: DIF-C items are harder ($d = -0.247$) and more discriminating ($d = 0.230$), distributed uniformly across subjects ($\chi^2$ $p = 0.32$). A difficulty $\times$ direction interaction ($\rho = -0.597$, $p < 0.001$) shows capability gains concentrate on hard items (88.7\% focal-favoring in hardest decile) while easy items erode (96.2\% reference-favoring in easiest decile).

Yen's Q3 analysis confirms that local dependence does not drive DIF: extreme LD correlates negligibly with subject-level C\% ($r = 0.09$, $p = 0.50$), while reasoning-heavy domains with low LD show higher DIF ($r = -0.36$, $p = 0.006$).

\subsection{Recommended DIF Audit Protocol}
\label{sec:disc_protocol}

These findings motivate a concrete benchmark maintenance protocol requiring only a binary response matrix and temporal cohort labels. We recommend: (1)~quarterly MH-DIF audits (or whenever $N \geq 80$ new models appear), using cross-subject matching; (2)~benchmark-wide C\% $> 20\%$ triggers item review, with items flagged ETS~C in consecutive audits labeled ``unstable''; (3)~graduated responses: flag-and-annotate, item rotation, or weighted scoring.

\section*{Limitations}

\paragraph{Methodological scope of MH-DIF.}
The Mantel-Haenszel procedure detects only \emph{uniform} DIF, where the odds ratio between cohorts is constant across ability strata. Logistic regression DIF analysis reveals an additional 24.8\% of items exhibiting non-uniform DIF ($|\beta_{\mathrm{interaction}}| \geq 0.5$), making the 31\% ETS~C estimate conservative. Future work should apply non-uniform DIF methods (e.g., logistic regression or IRT-based approaches) as the primary analysis to capture the full spectrum of item instability. Critically, MH-DIF is nonparametric and conditions on observed total score, not latent $\theta$---its validity does not depend on IRT model fit~\citep{holland1988mh}. Our Yen's Q3 analysis confirms that local dependence domains do not correlate with DIF domains ($r = 0.09$, $p = 0.50$), further supporting the robustness of the MH procedure in this setting.

\paragraph{Ability confounding.}
Although the temporal cohort ability gap is negligible (Cohen's $d = -0.17$, overlap $= 76.4\%$) and nearest-neighbor matching confirms that at most 2.4 percentage points of C\% are attributable to ability mismatch, within-family comparisons with large ability gaps (e.g., Llama-2 vs.\ Llama-3, $d = -0.99$) produce substantially inflated C\% (61.3\% unmatched vs.\ 28.3\% matched). Any application of MH-DIF to cohorts with larger ability differences must incorporate ability matching or stratification beyond the standard 5-strata procedure.

\paragraph{Sample size requirements.}
Our power analysis indicates reliable ETS~C detection requires $N \geq 80$ per cohort. The temporal cohorts (1,080 and 807 models) far exceed this threshold, but the GSM1K correlation analysis ($N = 33$) has limited statistical power (95\% CI $= [-0.71, -0.16]$). The negative correlation between contamination gap and DIF ranking advantage ($r = -0.478$) is exploratory and warrants replication with larger model samples.

\paragraph{Contamination vs.\ capability change.}
DIF analysis detects \emph{that} items function differently across cohorts but cannot determine \emph{why}. The differential signal may reflect contamination, capability change, training methodology shifts, or interactions among these factors. We present evidence that DIF captures something distinct from memorization (negative GSM1K correlation, semi-synthetic dose-response), but a definitive causal decomposition remains out of reach without access to model training data.

\paragraph{Benchmark scope.}
We analyze six METABENCH benchmarks (MMLU, WinoGrande, TruthfulQA, ARC, GSM8K, HellaSwag; 27,125 total items), all multiple-choice or short-answer benchmarks scored as binary correct/incorrect. The generalizability to open-ended generation benchmarks (e.g., AlpacaEval, MT-Bench), coding benchmarks (e.g., HumanEval), or benchmarks with non-binary scoring remains untested. The binary response format is a prerequisite for MH-DIF; extending to continuous or partial-credit scoring would require polytomous DIF methods.

\paragraph{Temporal cohort definition.}
We define cohorts by calendar year of model release (2023 vs.\ 2024), a coarse grouping that aggregates models with diverse training pipelines, data mixtures, and post-training procedures. Finer-grained cohort definitions (e.g., by training data cutoff, model family, or alignment method) might reveal more targeted patterns of item instability but would reduce per-cohort sample sizes and statistical power.

